WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:06.240
Can a mustache kill a movie? You betcha:&nbsp;
just ask Henry Cavill and Joss Whedon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:10.860
This is the story of Superman's brush with&nbsp;
infamy — and the fallout that followed.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:16.380
Kal-El, the Last Son of Krypton, famously&nbsp;
gains his powers from exposure to Earth's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:20.640
sun. Aside from a slew of abilities&nbsp;
that includes flight, super speed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.740
and x-ray vision, one thing that puts the&nbsp;
Man in Tights in a league of his own is his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:30.240
invulnerability. After all, he's not called&nbsp;
the Man of Steel for nothing. Naturally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.380
then, a standard razor should stand no&nbsp;
chance of clipping Clark Kent's facial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.640
hair to the baby-faced cleanliness that has&nbsp;
become standard for the fictional character.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:44.280
The uncertainty around Kal-El's shaving technique&nbsp;
became the theme of an ad campaign from Gillette&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:48.780
in 2013. Corresponding with the release of&nbsp;
Henry Cavill's premiere outing as Superman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:54.720
Man of Steel, the company went all out in an&nbsp;
attempt to answer this comic book riddle. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:00.300
company assembled a Justice League-level group of&nbsp;
nerds to present differing theories, from director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:05.880
Kevin Smith to Bill Nye, and fans were asked to&nbsp;
make a vote for their favorite theory online.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:09.060
"Other people have suggested a Kryptonite razor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.780
as if that's simple. Would you shave with&nbsp;
death, ladies and gentlemen? Of course not."

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.840
However, Gilette must have&nbsp;
missed the episode of Superman:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:21.360
The Animated Series in which Clark Kent gets&nbsp;
that perfect smooth trim using a mirror and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:25.860
his powerful heat vision — two products&nbsp;
that the company sadly doesn't sell.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:30.900
Since the first comic book appearance&nbsp;
of Superman in 1938's Action Comics #1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:35.100
the clean chin and upper lip have&nbsp;
been a staple for the character's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.760
visage. Although he has disguised&nbsp;
himself many times over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:44.400
the clean cut look is as much a part of&nbsp;
Supes' costume as the "S" on his chest.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.400
Clark Kent does have the&nbsp;
ability to grow facial hair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:51.720
though. Superman #139 features an&nbsp;
entire storyline around the effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:57.120
of Red Kryptonite weakening Kal-El's powerful&nbsp;
vision, resulting in his hair and fingernails&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.440
growing far more than is appropriate&nbsp;
for Kent's day job at the Daily Planet.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.900
So it's not that Superman can't grow a&nbsp;
sweet 'stache, but rather that audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:11.580
change their opinion regarding the true-blue&nbsp;
hero when he sports facial hair. Generally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:16.500
Superman donning a beard insinuates that the&nbsp;
bald-faced crimefighter has been absent for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:21.960
a period or aged considerably. As seen in&nbsp;
Superman III, something as insignificant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:27.960
as five o'clock shadow can even make Superman&nbsp;
look like an evil version of himself. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:34.620
the clean cut helps to fortify Clark Kent as the&nbsp;
responsible Boy Scout he traditionally represents.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:39.420
While Henry Cavill has been limited to filming&nbsp;
less than a minute of screen time as Superman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:45.360
in the last five years, the actor's early days in&nbsp;
the blue tights were highly productive. Making his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:51.120
debut in 2013's Man of Steel solo flick, Cavill&nbsp;
continued his time in the spotlight with Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:57.180
v Superman: Dawn of Justice in 2016. Powering&nbsp;
through that movie's poor critical reception,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:02.700
production for Cavill's next adventure as Supes&nbsp;
began less than a month later. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:06.780
unbeknownst to everyone involved&nbsp;
during those first days of filming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:10.680
Justice League was about&nbsp;
to unravel the entire DCEU.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:12.420
"Boo yah."

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:19.860
At the conclusion of Batman v Superman, Supes is&nbsp;
defeated by the monstrous Doomsday, and the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:24.840
half of Justice League is built around the rest&nbsp;
of the team bringing him back to life. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:29.580
the Man of Steel's screen time is limited compared&nbsp;
to his superpowered colleagues. Notably, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:36.000
even after being raised from the dead, Superman&nbsp;
maintains his babyface trim. Presumably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.900
every scene that Cavill shot for Justice&nbsp;
League during principal photography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:44.220
which wrapped in October 2016,&nbsp;
featured a clean-cut Clark Kent.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:49.860
The process of bringing Justice League to life&nbsp;
was tumultuous to say the least. Originally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:53.460
Warner Bros. had struggled to get the&nbsp;
movie going after first announcing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:59.940
pre-DCEU version in 2007. As production&nbsp;
finally loomed in 2016, Zack Snyder's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:04.680
original proposal of a two-part film was&nbsp;
nixed by the studio. Even during filming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:10.380
the backlash to the critically panned Batman v&nbsp;
Superman resulted in substantial rewrites and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:15.960
changes in producers. Still, the most significant&nbsp;
hit to the film occurred in post-production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:19.680
when Zack Snyder left the project due&nbsp;
to the sudden death of his daughter.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.820
After attempting to return to work,&nbsp;
Snyder opted to completely step away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:26.460
from Justice League. In his announcement, he said:

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:31.260
"In my mind, I thought it was a cathartic&nbsp;
thing to go back to work, to just bury&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:37.200
myself and see if that was the way through it.&nbsp;
The demands of this job are pretty intense. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:42.360
is all-consuming. And in the last two months,&nbsp;
I've come to the realization; I've decided to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:46.980
take a step back from the movie to be with my&nbsp;
family, be with my kids, who really need me."

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:51.000
As principal photography on Justice&nbsp;
League seemed to come to an end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:55.500
the cast dispersed into other projects.&nbsp;
Henry Cavill moved into production on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:59.700
action thriller Night Hunter, which failed&nbsp;
to garner much positive attention. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:05.160
it is notable for one particular reason: Cavill&nbsp;
had grown a scruffy beard for the project. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:09.780
when the actor shifted to his next film, Mission:&nbsp;
Impossible – Fallout, the choice was made to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:14.280
his character a mustache. Fallout director&nbsp;
Christopher McQuarrie later told the AV Club:

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:19.500
"Henry just finished [Night Hunter] and had&nbsp;
a big beard and the mustache and long hair.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:24.240
When he came to work, he had cut his hair but&nbsp;
still had that mustache and he asked, 'What do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.780
you think?' I thought about it for a second&nbsp;
and said, 'You know what, let's go for it.'"

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:32.820
Unfortunately, this off-hand decision would&nbsp;
later lead to significant problems when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:36.300
Cavill received a call to return&nbsp;
for reshoots on Justice League,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:39.600
three months into filming on&nbsp;
Mission: Impossible — Fallout.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:45.000
In the wake of Zack Snyder's departure from&nbsp;
Justice League, Warner Bros. and DC enlisted Joss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:50.400
Whedon to take over, who had previously helmed&nbsp;
Marvel's The Avengers and Avengers: Age of Ultron.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:53.100
"Avengers…"

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:59.820
With yet another creative mind now&nbsp;
embedded in the Justice League scuffles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:06.060
the studio agreed to two months and $25&nbsp;
million worth of reshoots — a significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:11.940
cost compared to the standard reshoot budget of&nbsp;
$6 million to $10 million and a couple of weeks.

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:17.100
Aside from scheduling conflicts with the movie's&nbsp;
stars, the studio had its own demands for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:23.040
reshoots that would add to this growing monster of&nbsp;
a film. First, the theatrical version was limited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:28.560
to a two-hour runtime, a demand made by Warner&nbsp;
Bros. CEO Kevin Tsujihara. More challenging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:32.760
the release date of the movie was&nbsp;
kept in its tight November 2017 spot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:38.340
despite these multiple obstacles. Warner&nbsp;
Bros. executives refused to consider any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:43.680
delays out of a fear of losing potential financial&nbsp;
bonuses ahead of an upcoming merger with AT&amp;T.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:50.340
The problem of Henry Cavill's mustache served to&nbsp;
distract from all the other drama surrounding the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:55.320
Justice League reshoots. The price tag Warner&nbsp;
Bros. faced for Justice League was approaching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.320 --> 00:07:00.150
$300 million with the added reshoot fund;&nbsp;
meanwhile, Paramount's Mission: Impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:06.360
– Fallout came in at an equally impressive $250&nbsp;
million. Obviously, neither studio wanted to face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:11.700
the risk of a continuity error, deal with the CGI&nbsp;
removal of Cavill's facial hair, or worst of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:16.440
have the actor wear a fake mustache. As&nbsp;
Christopher McQuarrie told the AV Club:

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:21.000
"I've worked with fake mustaches before.&nbsp;
You can get away with it for a shot or two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:23.160
but it doesn't work over the course of a movie."

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:28.500
Surprisingly, though, the director was open to&nbsp;
compromise. The Fallout crew tallied the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:33.360
of shots in which Cavill featured, and worked&nbsp;
out how much those shots would cost to reshoot:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:39.360
$3 million. McQuarrie then suggested that he shut&nbsp;
down production on the movie while Cavill grew his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:43.980
mustache back, while Warner Bros. simply would&nbsp;
pay Paramount for the loss. Paramount, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:48.780
refused to let McQuarrie do this. Because he was&nbsp;
under contract, Cavill was forced to keep his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:54.180
thick mustache during the Justice League reshoots.&nbsp;
In an unpleasant twist of fate, Fallout ended up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:59.520
having to shut down production anyways after&nbsp;
lead actor Tom Cruise broke his ankle on set.

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:05.040
As the dust settled on the studio war,&nbsp;
the task of disguising Cavill's mustache&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:09.960
landed in the laps of visual effects&nbsp;
artists — and it didn't turn out well.

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:14.820
"You won't let me live. You won't let me die."

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:19.140
The first leaked images of the cropped&nbsp;
mustache quickly took over the internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:23.460
to heavy mockery. In the aftermath,&nbsp;
an anonymous visual effects artist for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:27.360
DC opened up in a Reddit AMA that&nbsp;
has since been archived. Notably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:33.060
the unnamed VFX crewmate passed the blame for at&nbsp;
least one particularly terrible shot. They said:

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:39.480
"I'm not sure which other studio did that opening&nbsp;
shot on the cell phone but it's dreadful. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:43.980
shouldn't have been approved internally let&nbsp;
alone gone all the way to make it into the film."

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:47.880
Perhaps inevitably, Henry Cavill's botched lip job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:51.780
became one of the hottest topics&nbsp;
on social media. Cavill himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:56.400
however, enjoyed the process of becoming the&nbsp;
internet's favorite joke. He told Yahoo.com:

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:59.280
"I sure wasn't expecting that to happen. I love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:02.460
that it has. It's cool. I can&nbsp;
show my grandkids one day."

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:06.600
It didn't take long for Cavill to&nbsp;
stoke the flames of the meme sensation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:10.440
either. The actor took to his own&nbsp;
social media, sharing a picture of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:14.820
a strange-looking contraption from the set of&nbsp;
Mission: Impossible — Fallout. The caption read:

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:19.860
"Pictured above [...] is in fact the latest in&nbsp;
a series of weapons being designed by Warner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:25.500
Bros and Paramount Studios to combat the entity&nbsp;
known as "Henry Cavill's Moustache." [...] It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:30.420
is not a question of IF I should shave —&nbsp;
it is a question of how can we possibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:35.760
be victorious against such a beast without&nbsp;
bringing our own doom raining down upon us."

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:41.040
Sadly, Cavill later bade farewell to his&nbsp;
furry friend in a heartfelt Instagram post&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:46.260
that featured a long mustache montage backed&nbsp;
by Sarah McLachlan's "I Will Remember You."

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:51.180
"I can tell you this though,&nbsp;
I will remember him. Always."

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:56.400
The drama behind Justice League caught&nbsp;
up with the film upon its release. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:01.920
movie officially premiered in November 2017,&nbsp;
pulling in $96 million from the domestic box&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:06.840
office on opening weekend — short of the&nbsp;
$110 million the studio had projected.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:12.480
The lowest opening of any DCEU project at&nbsp;
that point, part of the failure was down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:17.100
to theatrical competition from Thor: Ragnarok&nbsp;
and Coco. It certainly didn't help, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:22.440
that the backstage confusion behind Justice League&nbsp;
translated so obviously onto the big screen.

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:27.780
Meanwhile, fans were anything but impressed that&nbsp;
Superman was absent from the majority of Justice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:32.760
League's theatrical cut, an issue made worse&nbsp;
by the movie's strange pacing. Worse still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:38.340
Cavill's uncanny CG face had apparently gone&nbsp;
unfixed for the cinematic debut, leading to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:44.220
even more insults regarding sloppy VFX. Fans&nbsp;
weren't sure what to expect from Justice League&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:50.220
after the disappointment of Batman v Superman&nbsp;
and the surprise success of Wonder Woman. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:55.860
with this movie, all of the DCEU's myriad&nbsp;
of problems seemed to come together at once.

00:10:55.860 --> 00:11:02.340
In an unprecedented maneuver from Warner Bros.,&nbsp;
the studio gave Zack Snyder another chance to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:08.340
finish his vision of Justice League. Folding to&nbsp;
a massive social media campaign calling on DC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:14.100
to "Release the Snyder Cut," WarnerMedia&nbsp;
subsidiary, HBO Max, handed the director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:20.160
$70 million to rebuild the film utilizing the&nbsp;
photography that he had already completed. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:25.620
a unique post-production process taking place&nbsp;
during the Covid-19 pandemic, the restructured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:32.400
4-hour Zack Snyder's Justice League was released&nbsp;
exclusively on the streaming platform in 2021.

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:36.840
Snyder pulled no punches discussing&nbsp;
the theatrical release of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:40.980
which he claims he has never watched.&nbsp;
At a Comic-Con event, he said:

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:43.260
"I would destroy the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:48.180
I would set it on fire before I would use&nbsp;
a single frame that I did not photograph."

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:51.960
And as for the infamous Superman 'stache?

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:55.740
"It's sad to think that that would have been the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:58.980
last view that people had&nbsp;
of Superman was just that."

